---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-for-teens`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D for Teenagers',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D for Teens', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D for Teenagers: Peak Bone Mass, Screen Time, and Sports Performance"
  description="Vitamin D in adolescence — peak bone-mass window, deficiency in indoor teens, sports and stress fracture risk, and dosing for teens 12-18."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-for-teens", label: "Vitamin D for Teens" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D for Teenagers</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Adolescence is the peak bone-mass window — roughly 90% of adult bone mass is
      acquired by age 18. Vitamin D and calcium adequacy in these years shapes
      lifetime osteoporosis and fracture risk. Teenage deficiency is common and
      rising — screen time and indoor lifestyles reduce sun exposure, and dietary
      calcium intake often falls short.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">RDA for teens</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Ages 9–18:</strong> 600 IU/day cholecalciferol (IOM/AAP).</li>
      <li><strong>Calcium:</strong> 1,300 mg/day — highest of any life stage.</li>
      <li><strong>UL:</strong> 4,000 IU/day.</li>
      <li>Endocrine Society suggests 1,000 IU/day for teens at risk of deficiency (dark skin, obesity, limited sun).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency prevalence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NHANES data: ~40% of US adolescents have 25(OH)D &lt; 20 ng/mL; higher in
      Non-Hispanic Black (~85%) and Hispanic teens (~65%). UK NDNS: 20% of adolescents
      deficient in winter. Screen time, indoor after-school hours, and dietary
      shifts all contribute.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sports and stress fracture</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Adolescent athletes — particularly in running, gymnastics, dance, and
      military-track preparation — have elevated stress-fracture rates. Vitamin D
      + calcium adequacy is one modifiable protective factor. See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-for-athletes" class="text-amber-700 underline">athletes page</a>.
      Screening 25(OH)D in adolescent athletes is reasonable.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Body image and eating disorders</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Restrictive eating patterns in adolescence (anorexia, orthorexia,
      restrictive weight cutting for sport) often produce low vitamin D and low
      calcium intake, adding bone-density loss to the many other harms. Screening
      and multidisciplinary care are essential.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guidance</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Encourage outdoor activity 15–30 minutes daily in warm months.</li>
      <li>Standard multivitamin providing 400–800 IU vitamin D is reasonable for teens with limited sun or dietary vitamin D.</li>
      <li>Ensure dairy or fortified plant-milk intake for calcium.</li>
      <li>Test 25(OH)D in symptomatic teens, athletes with stress-fracture history, or teens with restrictive dietary patterns.</li>
      <li>Winter supplementation (1,000 IU/day cholecalciferol) reasonable for northern-latitude adolescents.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Adolescence is the once-in-a-lifetime
      window for peak bone-mass accrual. Small habits established now (calcium
      intake, weight-bearing exercise, adequate vitamin D) pay off over decades.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-for-teens" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
